  "worked_solution": "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{d\\theta })}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{cot}\u2061(\\theta ).$ Also, since $r=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta =2\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dx}{d\\theta } & =-2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into our formula for the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(-\\operatorname{cot}\u2061(\\theta ))}{-2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{{csc}^{2}\u2061(\\theta )}{-2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\theta )} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{csc}^{3}\u2061(\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
